WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:06.100
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, CSUL.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:17.100
I would like to show you a very useful tip for simulating and for debugging your FSMs,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:24.100
and in general any module that has hierarchical structure.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:29.100
So let us first start by doing a regular simulation

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.300
of our test bench that was made for FSM example.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.780
The simulation window opens here

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.780
and here you see in this name pane

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.780
all the signals that are there inside of our FSM

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.580
underscore example TB.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:01:01.700
Here in the scope pane, you could see FSM itself, so you can, if there were multiple

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:10.300
levels of hierarchy, it would have been another level plus here, and you could open it further.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:19.460
But because there is not, we can take, basically by mouse, this FSM module, which is UUT, and

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.020
drag it to the name pane.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.100
In this example,

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:27.700
when we dragged it,

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.780
nothing is going to be shown on the simulation side of it

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.860
because during simulation,

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.300
none of these internal values

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.740
have been basically recorded.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.340
So in order to see,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.380
we have to restart the simulation,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.700
basically relaunch simulation,

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.700
And that is what we do using this little button

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.700
in the top portion of the manual.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:05.660
To be able to see better, we are going to expand all.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.980
We are actually going to turn a little bit more

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.980
zoom into the simulation.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.980
And for state, you see that values are going to one,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.220
one, two, three, and so forth, so and so forth.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.500
For the output, it's much easier for us to look at it

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.840
in a binary format, so it comes from all ones

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.840
to zero, one, one, one, and so and so forth.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.620
And if you can see here because enable,

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.080
so reset is one, sorry,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.440
we are in state zero.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.220
But if we move forward a little bit,

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:58.220
enable becomes zero and this current state is preserved.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.060
And after that, we move on.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.900
Here we see that current state is one

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.880
and the next state is two and so on and so forth.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.880
And with this method, basically you can keep observing

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:25.360
all the, let's call it, internals of either unit under test inside of test bench, or if

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:34.060
UUT is again hierarchical inside of each component of UUT.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.060
I hope this was helpful.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:35.660
Thank you.

